  "body_frame_note": "Every part is stated in its own rigid body's frame. At the nominal pose, the GZ18 free bodies keep the source model basis: +x forward, +y to the right, +z downward. Local z is measured from that body's origin, not from rail level; negative z means above the body origin. Wheels are not listed here; the exporter derives their datum, spin axis and rolling radius from the contact constants.",
  "sizes_note": "Schematic sizes chosen for display. They are not derived from mass or inertia and carry no physical meaning.",
  "placement_note": "Attachment points come from the mechanical definition's force elements: the primary suspensions at (±1.25, ±1.0, −0.438) on the bogie frames and (0, 0, −0.018) on the axlebox carriers, the air springs at (0, ±0.95, −0.638) on the bogie frames, the axlebox carrier bushings at (±0.77, ±1.055, −0.4335); the axlebox carriers pivot on their wheelsets at (0, ±1.0, 0). In the straight 60 km/h start-up state every force element has coincident end points, so the shapes are anchored on the bogie-frame side attachment points and sized schematically: the air springs stand on their attachment point, the primary springs are drawn on their attachment axis between the axlebox top and the raised side-beam end, the axlebox and arm sit on the pivot, and the axles end inside the wheel hubs. The rear bogie uses the same body-frame coordinates as the front one; the mechanical definition does not mirror it. All shapes and sizes are schematic.",
  "appearance_note": "An appearance names a display role (shell, glass, floor, structure, accent, dark), not a material; each viewer renders a role in its own way.",
  "naming_note": "End 1 is the +x end of the body frames, the model's front. Bogies, wheelsets and wheels are numbered from end 1; a label on one bogie names its end so both bogies can be labelled.",
  "display_bindings_note": "Display bindings name the bodies the viewer treats as carbody, bogie frames and carriers, in display order from end 1. They add no physics: wheel-to-carrier pairing, sides, datums and radii come from the record's wheel placements. A bogie's member list names the bodies drawn as its running gear; wheels follow their carrier and are not listed. Every carrier of the record must appear exactly once.",
